WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Heat-related illnesses happen when your body simply can't get rid of excess heat fast enough,

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.040
and they can range from mild discomfort to a life-threatening emergency.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.280
On the milder side, we have dehydration and heat cramps.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.799
If someone feels thirsty, has a dry mouth, or starts having painful muscle spasms,

00:00:18.799 --> 00:00:23.760
you need to move them to a cool place right away and have them rehydrate promptly.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.480
For those painful cramps, massaging the affected area and applying an ice pack

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.840
can really help alleviate the pain while they rest and recover.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.439
When things get more serious, we move into heat exhaustion.

00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:40.799
You'll want to look for signs like heavy sweating, nausea, dizziness, or even fainting.

00:00:40.799 --> 00:00:47.919
For this person, you must call 911, safely move them to a cool spot, and remove any excess clothing.

00:00:47.919 --> 00:00:52.560
If they are alert enough to drink, have them rehydrate with water and electrolytes,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.599
and use ice packs under their arms and on their groin

00:00:55.599 --> 00:00:58.720
to help bring their core body temperature down quickly.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.680
Finally, heat stroke is a true medical emergency

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.199
marked by a severe change in how responsive the person is.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:11.599
You must call 911 immediately and instruct someone to get an AED just in case their heart stops.

00:01:11.599 --> 00:01:16.959
While you wait for EMS to arrive, continue cooling them down as aggressively as possible,

00:01:16.959 --> 00:01:19.040
using the steps we've already covered.

